      "title": "1. The Purpose of Final Accounts for Different Stakeholders",
      "content": "All businesses need to keep records of their financial statements. Proper accounting gives managers **better financial control and planning**, and it can also be a **legal requirement**. Where there is a **separation of ownership and control**, much of a firm's finance may come from shareholders, so financial reporting is a way to **account for the money of the business**, whether it belongs to the owners, investors or lenders. Producing final accounts ensures that **all payments and receipts are officially accounted for**.\n\n**Final accounts** are the published annual financial statements that all limited liability companies are legally obliged to report. They consist of **two statements**:\n\n| Statement | What it reports | Time frame |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Profit and loss account** (income statement) | The **revenues and expenses** of a business | Over a specified accounting period (usually one year) |\n| **Balance sheet** (statement of financial position) | The value of **assets and liabilities** | At a **particular point in time** (a 'snapshot') |\n\n### Auditing and Legal Requirements\n* In most countries companies must have their final accounts **audited** by **independent and chartered accountants**, who certify the statements to be **accurate and truthful**.\n* **Incorporated businesses** (owned by shareholders) are legally obliged to produce final accounts to ensure **transparency** in the use of a company's funds.\n* There is **no universal method** of presenting final accounts, so there is some flexibility, as long as accounts follow the rules and regulations of the country.\n\n### Internal vs External Use\n* **Internal stakeholders** (executives and directors) use final accounts to **manage the business** and aid **strategic decision-making**.\n* **External stakeholders** use them to support **evaluative judgements**, such as the firm's ability to pay suppliers or repay financiers.\n\n### Stakeholder Uses of Final Accounts (p. 250)\n\n| Stakeholder | Purpose of final accounts |\n|---|---|\n| **Shareholders** | See where their money was spent and the **return on their investments**; decide whether to **hold, sell or buy (more of)** the company's shares |\n| **Employees** | Assess the likelihood of, or present a case for, **pay rises**; assess **job security** based on the financial position |\n| **Managers** | Judge **operational efficiency**; use financial analysis for **target setting and strategic planning** |\n| **Government** | The **tax authority** checks that businesses (especially large MNCs) pay the **correct amount of tax** on corporate profits |\n| **Financiers** | Commercial banks or business angels **scrutinize the accounts before approving any funds** (judging creditworthiness, Figure 17.1) |\n| **Suppliers** | Decide the extent to which **trade credit** should be given; need assurance that clients are **financially stable** so they will be paid |\n| **Potential investors** | Private and institutional investors use final accounts and **ratio analysis** to judge whether investment is worthwhile (profitability, **gearing**, **liquidity**) |\n| **Competitors** | **Compare financial performance** with their own |\n\n### Case Study 17.1: Enron\nEnron was a huge American energy company with annual sales of over **$100 billion** and over **20,000** workers. Its **dishonest accounting practices** caused it to go **bankrupt in December 2001**, and some top executives were jailed for accounting fraud. Its share price fell from over **$90 to less than $1** before bankruptcy.\n\n**Did you know?** **Luca Pacioli**, regarded as the **'Father' of accounting**, first wrote about the principles and ethics of accounting practices in **1494**.\n\n### Question 17.1: Nestlé (textbook case)\nNestlé, the world's largest food company (founded by Henri Nestlé in **1866**), is listed on the **Swiss stock exchange in Zurich**, employs over **352,000** people, competes with rivals such as **Mars** and **Cadbury's** in confectionery, and has owned a large share in **L'Oréal** since 1974. The textbook asks you to identify two stakeholder groups and **examine the importance of final accounts** to both (6 marks).",
