      "content": "### Balance Sheets of Sole Traders, Partnerships and Non-profit Entities\nThese are not owned by shareholders, so their balance sheets differ:\n* **Sources of finance differ**: limited companies can raise finance by selling **shares** and **debentures** (recorded under long-term liabilities); these do not appear for unincorporated businesses.\n* Funds invested by owners are recorded as **owners' equity** or **owners' capital** (not share capital).\n* **No dividends** appear. Instead, sole traders or partners may take funds out for personal use, known as **drawings**.\n\n### Limitations of Balance Sheets\n\n| Limitation | Explanation |\n|---|---|\n| **Static document** | The financial position may be very different in later periods, e.g. capital and reserves can change the next day if funds are used for expansion |\n| **Estimates only** | Figures are, at best, 'accurate' estimates. **Market value** may differ from **book value** (the value shown on the balance sheet); the true market value of a noncurrent asset is only known once it is sold |\n| **Incomplete** | No detailed breakdown of assets is shown |\n| **No universal format** | Firms use varying formats and include different items, making comparison difficult, even in the same industry |\n| **Not all assets included** | **Intangible assets** and **human capital** are often excluded, e.g. sports clubs do not include the market value of their players or coach. If employees are a firm's most valuable asset, the balance sheet does not accurately show the financial position |",
