      "id": "sec-3.4-depreciation",
      "title": "10. Depreciation: Meaning and Purpose (HL only)",
      "content": "**Appreciation** is the **increase** in value of noncurrent assets over time (property and land tend to rise in value). Most noncurrent assets, however, **depreciate**.\n\n**Depreciation** is the **fall in the value of noncurrent (fixed) assets over time**, caused by:\n* **Wear and tear**: assets such as computers and motor vehicles are used repeatedly, wear out and raise maintenance costs.\n* **Obsolescence**: newer and better products appear, so demand for, and the value of, existing assets falls (older vehicles, computers, smartphones, software).\n\nDepreciation **spreads the historic cost** (purchase cost) of fixed assets over their **useful lifespan**. The change in value is shown on the balance sheet under **'accumulated depreciation'**.\n\n### Why Depreciation Must Be Recorded\n1. **Calculate the value of a business more accurately**: depreciation reduces net assets to reflect the true value; revaluing appreciated assets increases net asset value.\n2. **Realistically assess the value of noncurrent assets over time**: historic costs are unlikely to equal current market values.\n3. **Plan for the replacement of assets**: provisions are made for buying new assets. Depreciation is also recorded on the **P&L account as an expense**.\n\nThe IB syllabus specifies **two methods**: (i) **straight line** and (ii) **units of production**. In practice it does not matter much which is used, but a business must be **consistent** to allow meaningful historical comparisons.\n\n### Key Variables\n* **Historic cost**: the purchase cost of the noncurrent asset.\n* **Life expectancy (lifespan)**: how long it will be used before replacement.\n* **Residual value (scrap value)**: an estimate of the asset's value at the end of its useful life. Many firms use **zero** because estimates may be inaccurate, though few assets lose all their value.",
