      "id": "sec-3.4-ethics",
      "title": "13. Principles and Ethics of Accounting, and the Key Concepts",
      "content": "Final accounts are important for two key reasons: **disclosure** and **accountability** of the financial matters of a business. Used properly and ethically, they reveal the **financial health and profitability** of a business. If profit has come from a **one-off sale of noncurrent assets** (e.g. land or property), there is likely to be **low profitability**. Although **window dressing is not illegal per se**, its **ethics** may be questioned.\n\n* Managers examine the **liquidity** position: the balance sheet reveals the firm's ability to meet daily running costs.\n* Both statements are used when seeking **external funding for growth** and to ensure **sustainability**.\n* Different countries have **different laws and cultural norms** for constructing final accounts.\n\n### Ethics of Accounting\n* **Ethical behaviour** is doing what society perceives to be the right thing. Wrongdoings, **even if not illegal, are unethical**.\n* **Professional regulatory bodies** monitor ethical standards, e.g. the **Australian Accounting Standards Board**, the **Accounting Standards Committee of Germany** and America's **Financial Accounting Standards Board**.\n* Accountants must be **open, transparent and honest** in recording and reporting all transactions; accounts should be **accurate and truthful** and meet the needs of the **tax authorities**.\n* Most businesses have an **annual audit** to ensure accounting is done with **integrity, objectively and ethically**.\n\nTOK: Can creativity in financial reporting ever be disregarded as being unethical?",
